WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
Retrieval is the decision layer that chooses what evidence reaches the model.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:08.100
A RAG system usually cannot send the entire knowledge base into the context window.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.235
It selects a limited set of chunks using query processing, filters, similarity search,

00:00:12.235 --> 00:00:16.240
keyword matching, ranking, reranking, source rules, and context limits.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.280
Those decisions strongly influence answer quality and safety.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.225
Retrieval should be constrained by user permissions, tenant scope, workflow purpose,

00:00:23.225 --> 00:00:25.355
source eligibility, and freshness requirements.

00:00:25.355 --> 00:00:29.245
A user should not receive a chunk just because it is semantically similar when the source

00:00:29.245 --> 00:00:30.030
is restricted,

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.240
expired, or outside the approved workflow.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.200
More retrieved text is not always better.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.840
Too much context can crowd out important evidence,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.505
introduce stale or conflicting material, increase cost,

00:00:39.505 --> 00:00:41.680
and make the answer harder to steer.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.080
Retrieval design should consider top-k choices, relevance thresholds, duplicate handling,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.540
source diversity, freshness preferences, and no-answer behavior when the evidence is weak.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.690
Context assembly is also an architectural boundary.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:56.665
The final prompt should separate trusted system instructions, application policy,

00:00:56.665 --> 00:00:58.890
user requests, and retrieved evidence.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.590
Retrieved chunks should be labeled as source material rather than treated as system

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:03.120
policy.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.205
That separation helps the model use evidence without confusing it with higher-priority

00:01:07.205 --> 00:01:07.955
rules.

00:01:07.955 --> 00:01:11.160
A mature private RAG system makes retrieval observable and tunable.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.380
Engineers should be able to see which chunks were considered, which filters were applied,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:15.930
why certain sources were selected,

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.715
and whether the answer was grounded in the retrieved material.

00:01:18.715 --> 00:01:21.040
Retrieval settings should be reviewed as requirements change.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.590
A workflow that supports exploratory research may need different ranking,

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:25.474
source diversity,

00:01:25.474 --> 00:01:29.280
and no-answer behavior than a workflow used for policy guidance or customer-facing

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:29.640
support.
